10:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L17",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 6, 1862, about noon through nightfall","place":"Jones Field and the western Shiloh battlefield; Crump’s Landing, Stoney Lonesome, the Shunpike, River Road, and Pittsburg Landing, Tennessee","result":"Sherman and McClernand counterattacked from Jones Field around noon or 12:30 p.m. Fourteen regiments advanced, surprised the Confederate left, and drove part of it back nearly half a mile before ammunition shortages forced a return to Jones Field. The attack delayed and further disorganized Hardee’s forces. North of the battlefield, Lew Wallace received Grant’s written order at about 11:30 a.m., allowed a meal break, and marched on the Shunpike toward Sherman’s former position. After learning after 2 p.m. that the United States line had moved, he countermarched, crossed to the River Road, and reached Grant’s army after dark following a march of at least sixteen miles","facts":[{"label":"United States counterattack","value":"About noon or 12:30 p.m. from Jones Field"},{"label":"Regiments documented in attack","value":"14 of 22 in Sherman’s and McClernand’s sector"},{"label":"Ground regained","value":"Nearly half a mile on McDowell’s front"},{"label":"Reason for withdrawal","value":"Regiments ran out of ammunition"},{"label":"Wallace’s division assembled","value":"About 6 a.m. at Stoney Lonesome"},{"label":"Written order received","value":"About 11:30 a.m."},{"label":"Route corrected","value":"After 2 p.m., following Rowley’s arrival"},{"label":"Wallace’s total march","value":"At least 16 miles in 7–8 hours; arrival after dark"}],"maps":[{"title":"Shiloh battlefield today","caption":"The NPS map identifies Jones Field, the Hamburg–Purdy Road, Snake Creek, Pittsburg Landing, and the roads north toward Crump’s Landing.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"Jones Field and the western battlefield","caption":"Sherman and McClernand had rallied near Jones Field after losing the Hamburg–Purdy Road line. Their noon counterattack moved south before returning to the field for lack of ammunition.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-april-6-grant-and-right.svg","alt":"Map of the Shiloh battlefield showing Jones Field and the western battle lines"},{"title":"Lew Wallace’s delayed march","caption":"Wallace chose the Shunpike toward Sherman’s original right, learned after 2 p.m. that the army had fallen back, countermarched, crossed to the River Road, and struggled through the Snake Creek bottomland.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/lew-wallaces-lost-division.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-lew-wallace-march.svg","alt":"Route map of Lew Wallace’s delayed march to Shiloh"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"The 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the battlefield, Pittsburg Landing, roads, camps, and positions that changed while Wallace marched.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of the Battle of Shiloh"}],"moments":[{"time":"0:42","audioTime":42,"title":"The episode places the Hornet’s Nest in context","detail":"The center’s defense was important, but it did not face the same concentration of Confederate forces as Sherman and McClernand on the western battlefield."},{"time":"3:53","audioTime":233,"title":"Sherman and McClernand reorganized at Jones Field","detail":"Seven brigades had suffered different levels of damage; only McDowell’s brigade remained fresh and nearly intact."},{"time":"6:07","audioTime":367,"title":"Sherman decided to counterattack","detail":"He judged that Confederate disorganization created an opportunity to delay the next assault and restore part of the United States position."},{"time":"7:50","audioTime":470,"title":"Fourteen regiments moved south","detail":"Around noon or 12:30, a ragged line advanced from Jones Field. About one-third of the formations in the sector did not participate."},{"time":"8:50","audioTime":530,"title":"The counterattack achieved surprise","detail":"McDowell’s and Marsh’s brigades formed the most stable parts of the advance and collected soldiers separated from other units."},{"time":"9:47","audioTime":587,"title":"The fighting divided into two results","detail":"Marsh fought a bloody standoff against Preston Smith and Patton Anderson, while McDowell drove Confederates back nearly half a mile."},{"time":"10:15","audioTime":615,"title":"Hardee threatened McDowell’s exposed flank","detail":"The flank threat halted further progress, and ammunition shortages then forced United States regiments back to Jones Field."},{"time":"11:04","audioTime":664,"title":"The counterattack delayed the Confederate left","detail":"It increased the confusion among Hardee’s intermingled formations and postponed another organized offensive."},{"time":"12:28","audioTime":748,"title":"Grant expected two reinforcing divisions","detail":"William Nelson approached on the east bank from Savannah, while Lew Wallace’s 3rd Division was near Crump’s Landing on the west bank."},{"time":"13:30","audioTime":810,"title":"Nelson’s march was delayed by mud","detail":"The head of his column did not reach the river opposite Pittsburg Landing until nearly sunset, too late for Sunday’s main fighting."},{"time":"15:09","audioTime":909,"title":"Wallace assembled at Stoney Lonesome","detail":"Around 6 a.m., he concentrated his division where roads led both to Pittsburg Landing and to Sherman’s original right."},{"time":"16:35","audioTime":995,"title":"Grant met Wallace at Crump’s Landing","detail":"At about 8:30 a.m., Grant told Wallace to remain ready to march when further orders arrived."},{"time":"17:55","audioTime":1075,"title":"Grant sent the written order","detail":"After reaching Pittsburg Landing around 9 or 9:30, Grant dispatched A. S. Baxter by steamer to order Wallace forward."},{"time":"18:35","audioTime":1115,"title":"Baxter could not find the division","detail":"Crump’s Landing was deserted. Wallace had left a horse but no staff officer to direct the messenger to Stoney Lonesome, costing about an hour."},{"time":"20:13","audioTime":1213,"title":"Wallace received the order at about 11:30","detail":"The written order was later lost, and witnesses disagreed over whether it specified Sherman’s right or the River Road toward Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"20:52","audioTime":1252,"title":"Wallace selected the Shunpike","detail":"He chose the road leading to Sherman’s original right rather than the River Road leading behind the army’s changing line."},{"time":"21:52","audioTime":1312,"title":"The division paused for a meal","detail":"Wallace allowed a half-hour dinner break after receiving the order, despite knowing that a battle was underway."},{"time":"24:21","audioTime":1461,"title":"Rowley reached Wallace after 2 p.m.","detail":"He explained that Sherman no longer held the Shunpike destination and that Grant wanted the division at Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"25:40","audioTime":1540,"title":"Wallace countermarched","detail":"He preserved the order of his column, adding about half an hour before taking a rough crossroad toward the River Road."},{"time":"27:07","audioTime":1627,"title":"Rawlins and McPherson overtook the column","detail":"They found Wallace’s division halted on the crossroad and urged faster movement; McPherson suggested moving artillery to speed the march."},{"time":"28:37","audioTime":1717,"title":"The column reached the River Road","detail":"It moved south through deep mud and standing water in the Snake Creek bottomland."},{"time":"29:34","audioTime":1774,"title":"The final defensive guns were audible","detail":"Grant’s siege guns signaled that the army was making its last stand near Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"30:16","audioTime":1816,"title":"Wallace arrived after dark","detail":"The 3rd Division completed at least sixteen miles in seven or eight hours despite beginning the day roughly five miles from the battlefield."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Directed Wallace to march and sent multiple staff officers to find and accelerate the 3rd Division"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman","command":"5th Division","subordinates":"Organized the Jones Field counterattack with McClernand"},{"commander":"John A. McClernand","command":"1st Division","subordinates":"Sent staff through the disorganized regiments and advanced beside Sherman"},{"commander":"John McDowell / C. C. Marsh","command":"Principal counterattack brigades","subordinates":"McDowell on the right; Marsh in the center; rallied elements joined both"},{"commander":"Lew Wallace","command":"3rd Division","subordinates":"Three brigades marching from Stoney Lonesome to Pittsburg Landing"},{"commander":"John A. Rawlins / James B. McPherson / William R. Rowley","command":"Grant’s staff","subordinates":"Located Wallace, clarified the route, and urged the division forward"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"Confederate left","subordinates":"Directed resistance to the counterattack and threatened McDowell’s right flank"},{"commander":"Robert Trabue","command":"Brigade","subordinates":"Four cohesive regiments facing McDowell with elements of other brigades"},{"commander":"Preston Smith / Patton Anderson","command":"Brigades","subordinates":"Opposed Marsh in a prolonged standoff"}]}],"people":[{"name":"William T. Sherman","role":"Proposed and directed the first substantial United States counterattack of the day","side":"United States","image":"/images/william-t-sherman.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670507/"},{"personId":"john-a-mcclernand","role":"Reorganized parts of the 1st Division and advanced with Sherman from Jones Field"},{"name":"Lew Wallace","role":"Led the 3rd Division along the wrong approach for the army’s changed position and arrived after dark","side":"United States"},{"name":"John A. Rawlins","role":"Grant’s senior staff officer who dictated the first written order and later overtook Wallace’s column","side":"United States"},{"name":"James B. McPherson","role":"Accompanied Rawlins and helped reorganize Wallace’s marching column","side":"United States"},{"name":"William R. Rowley","role":"Explained after 2 p.m. that Sherman had withdrawn and Grant wanted Wallace at Pittsburg Landing","side":"United States"},{"name":"A. S. Baxter","role":"Carried the written order from Pittsburg Landing but lost time finding Wallace’s division","side":"United States"},{"name":"William “Bull” Nelson","role":"Led the Army of the Ohio division delayed by mud on the east bank of the Tennessee","side":"United States"},{"name":"William J. Hardee","role":"Directed the Confederate left against Sherman and McClernand’s counterattack","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/william-j-hardee.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670314/"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing on the Tennessee River in 1862","caption":"Grant expected Wallace’s fresh division to reach this area by midday; it arrived after dark.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"Mud, wooded roads, and flooded bottomland slowed both reinforcement columns approaching the battlefield.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battle-print.jpg","alt":"Historical print of the Battle of Shiloh","caption":"Sherman and McClernand’s counterattack kept much of the Confederate army occupied on the western battlefield.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 119","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/119-shiloh-part-the-ninth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Lew Wallace’s Lost Division","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/lew-wallaces-lost-division.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Shiloh maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pittsburg Landing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"}]}}]}]
